 and attention to individual readiness.","Stop 45-Degree Cut and Reaccelerate for pain, guarding, slipping, repeated loss of alignment or balance, unsafe lane traffic, or a material decline in the assigned movement quality."]},{"sectionKey":"accessibility","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/about-us/position-statements/youth-training-and-long-term-athletic-development/","sourceTitle":"Youth Training and Long-Term Athletic Development","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["Youth development should be individualized, progressive, and based on current physical and psychosocial readiness rather than a fixed exercise-card level.","Make 45-Degree Cut and Reaccelerate accessible with a walk-through, shorter approach or projection, lower speed, visible floor arrows, longer reset, and a simpler prerequisite task; do not label the exercise itself beginner or advanced."]},{"sectionKey":"media","sourceUrl":"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/171780?expand=PrivacyEnhancedMode&hl=en","sourceTitle":"Embed videos and playlists","sourcePublisher":"YouTube Help","sourceKind":"manufacturer_instruction","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["YouTube supports privacy-enhanced embedding through youtube-nocookie.com.","The supplied links are candidate demonstrations only. oEmbed availability was checked, but exact sequence, safety, cue quality, captions, full content, reviewer identity, and approval remain pending."]},{"sectionKey":"identity","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/education/articles/kinetic-select/run-and-cut/","sourceTitle":"Run and Cut","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["The NSCA run-and-cut exercise uses a five-yard approach, deceleration into a cut step, and immediate acceleration approximately 45 degrees away.","45-Degree Cut and Reaccelerate matches that planned approach-cut-exit identity when approach, plant, angle, and immediate acceleration are explicit."]},{"sectionKey":"taxonomy","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12179697/","sourceTitle":"Center of mass direction and speed during a 45-degree change of direction task performed with maximal effort","sourcePublisher":"Frontiers in Sports and Active Living","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":86,"claims":["A maximal 45-degree task shares direction change across approach, execution, and following steps.","Classify the full approach-to-exit sequence, plant side, sidestep technique, approximate angle, and planned cue state rather than only the named plant."]},{"sectionKey":"anatomy","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39574461/","sourceTitle":"Effect of foot strike patterns and angles on the biomechanics of side-step cutting","sourcePublisher":"Frontiers in Bioengineering and Biotechnology","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":83,"claims":["Forty-five-degree sidestep cutting produces ankle, knee, hip, ground-reaction-force, and lower-limb muscle demands during loading and propulsion.","Represent ankle and subtalar stabilization, knee and hip force acceptance, pelvis and trunk control, and the lower-limb musculature that brakes and reaccelerates the body."]},{"sectionKey":"biomechanics","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12179697/","sourceTitle":"Center of mass direction and speed during a 45-degree change of direction task performed with maximal effort","sourcePublisher":"Frontiers in Sports and Active Living","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":86,"claims":["At maximal effort, the execution step contributed only part of the 45-degree direction change while approach and following steps also redirected center-of-mass velocity.","The card must coach distributed braking and a coordinated following step rather than a single isolated plant claimed to create the entire angle."]},{"sectionKey":"difficulty","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8363537/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical Determinants of Performance and Injury Risk During Cutting: A Performance-Injury Conflict?","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":92,"claims":["Cut performance and injury-related loading depend on interacting whole-body mechanics, angle, speed, and task strategy.","Score technical complexity and physical body-mass demand directly, derive overall as their maximum, and retain supervision, consequence, impact, coordination, and work capacity as separate planning dimensions."]},{"sectionKey":"load_fatigue_recovery","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9474351/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical and Neuromuscular Performance Requirements of Horizontal Deceleration: A Review with Implications for Random Intermittent Multi-Directional Sports","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":90,"claims":["Horizontal deceleration exposes athletes to high eccentric forces, and braking demands rise with momentum and task constraints.","Log approach speed, side, contact count, exit distance, surface, total cutting and sprint exposure, and technique degradation; bodyweight does not mean low load."]},{"sectionKey":"constraints","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8016420/","sourceTitle":"Reliability of the Cutting Alignment Scoring Tool (CAST) to Assess Trunk and Limb Alignment During a 45-Degree Side-Step Cut","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Sports Physical Therapy","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":84,"claims":["Reliable qualitative observation of 45-degree side-step cutting depends on standardized task geometry and visible alignment.","45-Degree Cut and Reaccelerate requires a clear approach, cut marker, exit lane and runoff, reliable traction, no cross traffic, and views that show trunk, hip, knee